From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lisa Desktop <lisa-desktop@lisaos.dev>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 02:26:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Lisa Desktop may photograph its own screen (lisa-os#266)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

GNOME 50 gates org.gnome.Shell.Screenshot on DBusSenderChecker, which
allows exactly two well-known bus names: org.gnome.SettingsDaemon.
MediaKeys (a person pressing Print) and org.freedesktop.impl.portal.
desktop.gnome (the interactive-consent path). Every other caller gets
"Screenshot is not allowed". So on the reference device nothing the OS
itself ships can capture the screen — not for the owner, not for CI on
real hardware, not for a bug report — and several verification claims
this week had to end with "appearance is unverified".

Adding a Lisa bus name to that allowlist would not fix it: a well-known
name is claimed first-come on a bus every process on the seat can reach,
so it would grant screen capture to whoever asked for the name first.
Authorisation obtainable by asking nicely is not authorisation.

Identity comes from the transport instead (ADR-0033). The bus daemon
reports the peer's pid and uid from SO_PEERCRED; /proc/<pid>/exe is the
kernel's link to the inode that process runs; the inode's ownership says
whether anyone but root could have put it there. A caller is authorised
only if it runs a regular file directly inside /usr/lib/lisa/bin that is
owned by uid 0 and not group- or other-writable, in a directory that is
likewise root-owned and unwritable, under the uid the shell runs as.
That directory is installed by the image and is read-only to every
non-root process. The Lisa CLI's runtime-channel copy under
/var/lib/lisa-apps is owned by the user, and is therefore refused: an
unprivileged update channel does not get to hand this to arbitrary code.

Upstream's checks are untouched — the Lisa path is only consulted after
DBusSenderChecker has already said no, so everything GNOME refuses today
it still refuses. Interactive screenshot, SelectArea and FlashArea keep
their own unmodified checks; this covers only the three capture methods
that go through _createScreenshot.

A Lisa-authorised capture always flashes and plays the shutter sound,
whatever the `flash` argument said, and logs the calling binary and pid.
A capture nobody can see is a camera with the light disabled, and the
shell is the only participant a caller cannot rewrite.

This authorises a binary, not an intention: whoever can run
/usr/lib/lisa/bin/lisa can reach what it exposes. That is deliberate —
a guardrail sits between the model and the machine, never between a
person and their own machine (ADR-0029, ADR-0030). Provenance,
screen.once and the Ledger entry belong to the CLI's guard, which is
why the capture path is narrowed to one auditable binary rather than
left open to every process on the bus.
